  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThe deep trough responsible for our rainy and cold Halloween weekend \nhas moved into the Great Basin, evident on mid-level water vapor \nsatellite imagery. On the back side, a thermal ridge axis sets up \nover California as temperatures warm today around 5-10 degrees \nover Sunday with clear skies. This upper level ridge is somewhat \ncompressed, situated between the aforementioned trough over the \nGreat Basin and a deepening low off the coast of Oregon. This \nridge will likely be pushed out before it can be too helpful, \nwith the Oregon low moving into Northern California by Tuesday \nnight. Despite the significant warm up today, temperatures will \nhold steady with a lackluster marine layer into Wednesday given \nthe quick changes in the upper level pattern.\n\nGusty onshore winds accompany the Oregon low on Tuesday as the \ngradient tightens over SoCal. Winds will pick up over the coastal \nwaters as early as late morning on Tuesday, with inland spread \nthrough the afternoon. Gusts will reach up to 25-30 mph over the \nwaters and as high as 20 mph for coastal and valley areas. Winds \nwill decrease a bit as the low moves inland, likely calming by \nsunrise Wednesday morning. Some models continue to support light \nrainfall Wednesday morning for far southern San Diego County as \nthis trough moves across, but chances only sit around 15-25% at \nthis point. Still, it wouldn't be surprising if some light wetting\nrain was observed early Wednesday morning, primarily for SD \nCounty south of the 56. \n\nAs previously mentioned, temperatures won't be impacted much by the \nlow as it swings across, likely out of California by peak heating \nWednesday afternoon. As the low moves off, a positively tilted \nridge will move ashore, bringing subsidence for Thursday/Friday as\nareas west of the mountains finally warm back above normal. Late \nweek into early weekend, another significant pattern change will \naffect much of the western United States, with a very large ridge \nstretching from northern Canada down into the Desert Southwest. As\nhigh pressure moves over the Four Corners Saturday, offshore \nwinds will increase for most of California, bringing about well-\nabove-normal temperatures for areas west of the mountains. These \ndry, downsloping winds will push temperatures to the 80s for even \ncoastal communities, around 10-15 degrees above normal, and minor \nto moderate Heatrisk will return for the western portions of the \narea. Current forecast indicates that by Saturday, temperatures\nnear the coast may be even hotter than the Coachella Valley. \nHumidity will plummet, potentially as low as 15% for Saturday and \nSunday afternoons. While there is still some disagreement in the \nplacement of the ridge for the weekend, which will drastically \ninfluence the Santa Ana wind strength, most models remain in favor\nof the significant warming and stronger easterly winds this \nweekend. Should this pan out, it will be quite the contrast to the\npast gloomy Halloween weekend.",
